# Book Two — Wonder About Pi(e)
# Working arc for chambers 14 onward. Book One (01–13) carried an unspent wish to a
# furrow kept open "for whatever arrives next without a receipt". Book Two is what arrives.
# Plot is flexible; Boof's characterization is not; the Egg stays an open question.
#>book_two
#:story #!book-two #!working

^"book_two"{
  .title: "Wonder About Pi(e)"
  .status: "working"
  .chambers: "14 →"
  premise: "The Genie paid for Boof's wish with everything he had left, plus one Thursday. The Thursday goes missing from every person's week and stays in the field. On the day nobody kept, someone arrives through the warm furrow without a receipt — a figure in a few plain lines under a hat that never lands."
  shared_question: "What happens to a wish after it leaves the person who made it?"
  book_question: "Can a figure made to signify something become a person?"
  &boonhonk: ~".spw/story-boonhonk.spw#lore_land_story_boonhonk"
  &egg: ~".spw/surfaces/dragon-egg.spw#dragons_egg"
}

^"host_vector"{
  .note: "The plot the book's people are pursuing. WAPboy crosses it at a right angle (story-boonhonk#trope_physics)."
  feast: "Bonk City's Feast of the Uneven Slice at the Turning of the Year, where the one destined to spill exactly one pie is foretold to come to the table."
  hunt: "The city wants the destined one found before the feast — some to stop the spill, some to crown it."
  floating_title: "The chosen title floats like the hat. It lands on the wrong person for one scene at a time without becoming false."
  orthogonal: "The figure attends to whoever the feast overlooks: an ant whose torch went out, a porter holding a gate, the person who washes the plates."
}

^"threads_paid"{
  .note: "Book One plants that Book Two collects. Collect by placement, never by explanation."
  thursday: "chs 03, 05, 08, 12, 13 — a small wish costs a coincidence and a Thursday; ch14 is the Thursday."
  coincidence: "ch07 — every spent wish owes a coincidence to a stranger; ch12 — a stranger would receive a good coincidence. Left unassigned on purpose."
  furrow: "ch12 — one warm furrow kept open, always, for whatever arrives next without a receipt; ch14 — something does."
  hat: "ch13 — a hat at a person's height, touching nothing, farther down the road each time she looked."
  slice: "ch06 → ch10 — the warm BoonWAP slice Boof has never eaten; still warm in ch14. Carried, never explained."
  button: "ch06 — somebody in the plaza lost a button; ch15 — the figure retrieves it from under the parcel gate."
  bell: "ch01 far cart bell → ch13 nearby → ch14 arrives on the crier's cart."
}

^"chambers"{
  c14: {
    title: "The Thursday Nobody Kept"
    signal: "first_signal + field_signal + orthogonal_signal (story-boonhonk#teaser_grammar)"
    beats: "no person is in the day → the berries correct a quarter turn → ruled ant-streets → the hat above the furrow, a drawing under it facing ninety degrees away → a dead torch relit from the end of a line arm; the arm stays shorter → straight across country, hedge left with a rectangular gap → Friday arrives with everyone in it → the crier announces the feast; mistakes him for a scarecrow; writes that she saw a hat"
    remainder: "a straight ant-street with a torch that was a little of somebody's arm"
  }
  c15: {
    title: "Child, Parcel, or Door"
    signal: "second_signal — records disagree; all three remain valid"
    beats: "a straight line comes out at the parcel gate where no road arrives → the porter must shut the gate before the storm; the figure fits under the lintel and the hat does not → the porter holds the gate; the feast flour is spoiled; he signs the damage in his own name → the button → gate-book (parcel), repair log (door), night-school roll (child) → Mx. BoneWAP posts all three → by one o'clock the city decides the destined one is the dog"
    remainder: "three receipts pinned side by side; a lintel marked where a hat very nearly touched it"
  }
  c16: {
    .status: "next stone — 2026-11-13"
    title_seed: "The Second Place"
    seed: "Boof wears the floating title for one scene at BoonWAP's table. The figure, at the back, dries plates beside the washer-up nobody has asked the name of (story-boonhonk#boonhonk_anchor_last_slice). If the pie is never smaller, why does the person serving it still get tired?"
  }
}

^"sightings"{
  .note: "Play loop (book/scripts/modules/sightings.mjs): nine sightings, nine strokes of the plain drawing. Noticing one — a margin rumour surfacing, or a prose passage read — earns a stroke; the drawing never earns a name."
  margins: "sighting-sand 03 head · sighting-records 04 body · sighting-hat 06 hat · sighting-corners 09 left leg · sighting-orthogonal 11 right leg · sighting-scent 12 left eye"
  prose: "road 13 left arm · furrow 14 right arm (drawn shorter: the torch) · button 15 right eye"
  where_seen: "a settle card on the chapter (switch: sightings, default on); the home hat drawer; the desk's ?[sightings]"
  extend: "a later sighting adds no strokes — nine is whole. New sightings can redraw (a moved line, a missing dot) once the story earns a choice."
}

^"exactly_one_pie"{
  .note: "A small game at /pie/ (book/scripts/home/pie-game.mjs, book/styles/home/pie.css; PLAN.E L28). The prophecy as a thing you can hold."
  premise: "Someone is destined to spill exactly one pie. A figure in a hat carries one, level, up the lanes of Bonk City while the city streams down at him."
  orthogonal: "He never goes the way the scene goes; he steps across it, one lane-length at a time — a line segment, with the ghost of where he was (story-boonhonk.spw#trope_physics orthogonal_motion, quest)"
  drawing: "always the same plain ink drawing, in every binding: one circle, one rectangle, four lines, two dots; the hat lags behind him and never falls, not even at the spill"
  wobble: "every step tilts the pie, steps in a hurry most; puddles slip it; it settles if left alone — feeling as tilt and spacing, not as a face"
  boof: "crosses the lanes on her own business now and then; brushing past her steadies the pie. She is the materially specific animal beside the drawn figure; she works for the land, and helps in passing"
  cuts: "silent-film title cards between places: the parcel gate in a storm; the lanes, which curve for everyone; the plaza, where the pie holds court; the long table, where a second place is set — the empty place is the only way through"
  the_end: "always exactly one spill; the card says EXACTLY ONE., what the land would say about the cause, and how far he carried it. The prophecy holds; the distance is the reader's"
  naming: "he is never named on the page; the game calls him a figure in a hat"
  under_the_hat: "it is not always him. The chosen title floats like the hat and may be worn by the wrong person for a scene (story-boonhonk.spw chosen_one): each carry the hat settles on someone — the figure in plain lines, the porter from the parcel gate (steady, slow), a feast clerk (quick, nervous), the Genie off-season (glides), a clown (not to be trusted with a pie) — and at a cut it may move on. The others are drawn in their materials; only he is plain ink"
  wap: "clowns love WAP — Bonk City's word for a pie in the face, after the sound it makes. Clowns drift toward the pie; step into one and WAP: it gets the pie square, loves it, and the pie on the plate is no smaller (one pie that remains whole after being served). A WAP jostles the plate; it is not a spill. A clown carrying the pie that tips too far gives itself WAP, which counts as the spill"
  sound: "a WAP is heard: a synthesized splat and two horn honks (Web Audio, no files), only after the reader starts a carry; a toggle remembers"
  quinary_five: "Mr. BoonWAP, a sentient pie of the quinary five (spwashi.com, RPG Wednesday: the quinary story bible), holds court at the head of the lanes: clowns flock, every pie finds an eye (a wider reach), and he lobs slices of himself into theirs; he leaves still talking after the room has moved on. Mr. BaneWAP, his foil, meddles with an announced scheme — a rented wind that shoves the carry a lane, a tax that doubles every step's tilt, the lanes re-let under new management — and, a proper cartoon villain, leaves a visible off switch on the way through; step on it, or throw at it in the gold moment, and he is foiled. They never share the stage; the plaza leans boon, the rented court bane"
  throw: "a slice up the lane (↑ W Space, swipe up, the throw button); the pie on the plate is never smaller, the next slice takes a second. A clown or switch in the lane wears a ring that turns gold 120–230 px out: a throw then is Perfect — the WAP counts twice and steadies the pie; a switch is foiled from afar. Point-blank lands, and jostles"
  flour: "flour never spills the pie: it slows the carry (a drag that eases off) and pushes it back in the story — 70 cobbles back along this place, or, early in a place, back to the last; a five-place track under the HUD shows where the carry is"
  rows: "the city comes in rows; each row leaves a way through no more than one lane from the last; drifting things stay within a fifth of a lane of home; hazards hit a little smaller than they look, friends a little larger; the pace builds from 150 by the minute and the place, and rows keep a set distance so the time to react holds"
  levels: "gate (Chapter Fifteen, the parcel gate) · lanes (Chapter Six, the Fool's Labyrinth) · plaza (Chapter Six, the pie at the table) · court (Chapter Eleven, the wish that would not finish) · table (Chapter Fifteen, a second place set)"
  lore: "what the pies let slip (book/content/world/pie-lore.json): Mr. BoonWAP says one true thing about the land each time he holds court; Mr. BaneWAP, foiled, blurts one. Lines unlock with what the visitor has found across the site (chapters read, a chapter read, wonders kept, sightings, tellings heard) and done in the lanes (WAPs, perfects, foils, the furthest place reached). Each grounds itself in a chapter. The page keeps the said, the ready, and the locked with how to unlock them. Rules: the Egg stays a mystery; the figure under the hat is never named; the warm slice is never explained; 'there are five of us; everyone counts four' is as near as anyone comes"
  caption: "places and the pies' words are said under the screen, like subtitles; a line stays, quieter, until the next — never across the carry"
  aim: "throws do not home: a needle sweeps from the pie and a slice flies where it points (a swipe sets its own angle). A target's ring turns gold when the needle lines up with its face. Perfect: dead centre, off a wall, or off a ramp. Bronze posts bend a slice; walls bounce it (each bounce costs reach); crown-wasps and gate-pigeons snatch it; anything solid takes it with a splat; a rented wind drifts it"
  aura: "the pie in hand stays whole because an aura keeps replenishing it, from two ingredients: flour (the crust) and boonberries (the filling, teal and amber lit from within, in the lanes). Every slice draws on both; a wasted slice costs more; run either dry and no new slices come, and a thin pie tips more easily. Mr. BoonWAP's benevolence: filling poured back in, a steadying word, wasted slices caught"
  flour_and_ramps: "flour comes in bags (crust, a slowdown, a setback in the story) and piles (a little crust, a little slower). A pile is a ramp: a slice over it is lofted clean over the next thing; piles in a row stack the loft. A slice thrown into a bag bursts it into a pile — composing a ramp — and is not wasted"
  legend: "the page explains every thing with sprites drawn by the game's own hand: keep clear, slows you, go toward, two of the quinary five, under the hat"
  segues: "pie-segues.mjs: where a chapter reaches a level's place, a door at the section's end carries the pie from there (?scene= &from=); the ending points into the chapter where the level happens, and the way back returns to the very section the game was found in, where a note says how the carry went"
}

^"pie_voices"{
  .note: "How Mr. BoonWAP and Mr. BaneWAP speak, in the chapters and in Exactly One Pie, so that each line they say can carry a piece of the land's lore (canon of the quinary five: spwashi.com play/rpg-wednesday, the quinary story bible)."
  boonwap: "talks past the room: answers questions nobody asked, takes a nod for agreement, beams a long way; and in every speech one true thing about the land. He misses what the room is telling him — the server's wrist, the washing-up — and the prose lets the reader see it (ch6). He is generous without noticing what it costs or what it fixed (ch12)."
  banewap: "announces: a scheme is not a scheme until it is declared to someone who did not ask. Every scheme carries its off switch — a clause he wrote himself and never reads (ch11). Foiled, he always says one thing more than he means to (“The crown was wasps.”). Re-sweetened at the bridge, he schemes from habit, and asks, in the form of a threat, to be treated kindly (ch12)."
  in_the_chapters: "ch6 the pie at the table (never smaller: only arithmetic); ch11 the announcement and the clause, the blurt; ch12 the bridge (I have always said so; bitter starting tomorrow unless); ch15 the long table (the second place is for whoever it was set for)"
  in_the_game: "the game's lore lines (book/content/world/pie-lore.json) are what they say off the page in the same shapes: BoonWAP's while he holds court, BaneWAP's when foiled; each line grounds itself in the chapter where its shape, or its fact, can be read"
  rule: "neither of them decodes anything: the Egg stays a mystery, the figure under the hat is unnamed, the warm slice unexplained; the nearest either comes is BoonWAP's 'there are five of us; everyone counts four'"
}

?"open"[
  "Who receives the good coincidence the small wish owed a stranger?"
  "What does the hat remember?"
  "Whose arm-length of line is burning in the ant-street, and does it grow back?"
  "Is the empty shell at the archive still warm because of what left it, or what is coming?"
  "Who is destined to spill exactly one pie — and is destiny something you can be mistaken for?"
]

~"guards"{
  wapboy: "Never decode the drawing in prose. The name WAPboy does not appear in chapter text until a choice earns it."
  egg: "Do not say what hatched, or whether anything did. The shell stays warm and empty."
  fool: "Sidelined. Book Two does not lean on the Fool."
  boof: "Works for the land, not for pay or fruit; appreciates blueberries without liking them; nose before name."
  compass: "No cardinal directions — dawnward, hearthward, duskward, by day or destination."
  ledger: "Records appear as comedy and as care, never as the engine."
}

^"plates"{
  .rule: "A Book Two plate is a painted scene with the figure drawn into it in plain lines — the mismatch is the canon (story-boonhonk#wapboy scene_relation)."
  .status: "study — drawn over 2026-03-01 seed paintings until a painted plate is grounded"
  p14: "lone tree under a teal storm; a stream bends, the hat goes straight up the slope"
  p15: "Bonk City's snowed back lanes at night; the figure with a parcel tag, a lit seam like a door, a button in one hand"
}
